      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find a number that is a multiple of every integer, we need to understand what a multiple of an integer is. A number \\( n \\) is a multiple of an integer \\( k \\) if there exists some integer \\( m \\) such that \\( n = m \\cdot k \\).\n\nIn this case, we want a number \\( n \\) that is a multiple of every integer \\( k \\). To achieve this, we need to find a number that can divide every integer equally. But there is no such number because no number can satisfy the condition of being divisible by every integer in a consistent way. In other words, if a number \\( n \\) were possible, it would have to be zero because zero is divisible by every integer. This is because \\( 0 = 0 \\cdot k \\) for any integer \\( k \\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
